  "explanation": "<p><strong>Why saddles dominate.</strong> At a critical point in $d$ dimensions the Hessian has $d$ eigenvalues. A local minimum needs <em>all</em> of them positive. If signs were independent and equally likely, that is one outcome in $2^d$ — so for any realistic $d$ almost every critical point has at least one negative direction, i.e. is a saddle.</p><p><strong>Why the 3rd is backwards.</strong> At a saddle the true gradient is zero, so full-batch gradient descent has nothing to move it and stalls. SGD's mini-batch gradient is almost never exactly zero, and that noise knocks it off the saddle. <em>Noise is the escape mechanism</em>, so the method with more noise escapes more easily.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th matters in practice.</strong> You rarely land exactly on a critical point. What actually costs you is the large, nearly flat region around it, where the gradient is tiny but non-zero and progress crawls for many epochs.</p><p><strong>Why a saddle needs two dimensions.</strong> On the left, a one-parameter loss has only two kinds of critical point: the curve turns up or it turns down. On the right, the red point is a minimum along $\\theta_1$ and a maximum along $\\theta_2$ — the gradient is zero, yet an escape route exists. <strong>Full-batch gradient descent cannot find it, because the thing it follows is exactly zero</strong>; SGD's noise pushes it off the ridge and down the amber direction. In $d$ dimensions there are $d$ such signs to get right, which is why genuine minima are rare and saddles are everywhere.</p>",
