  "num": 28,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p>$\\mathcal{D}^2 = (x_i - x_j)^{\\top} M (x_i - x_j)$ is only a valid squared distance if it is never negative, i.e. if $M$ is <strong>positive semi-definite</strong>. Optimising $M$ directly would mean projecting back onto the PSD cone after every step — an awkward constrained problem.</p><p>Writing $M = W^{\\top}W$ makes the constraint <em>structural</em>: for <em>any</em> real $W$ whatsoever, $z^{\\top}W^{\\top}Wz = \\lVert Wz \\rVert^2 \\ge 0$ automatically. So $W$ can be optimised freely by ordinary gradient descent and the constraint can never be violated.</p><p><strong>It is the constraint, not the cost, that is the reason</strong> — and note the reading this gives: learning a Mahalanobis distance is the same thing as learning a linear embedding $W$ and using plain Euclidean distance in it.</p>",
