  "explanation": "<p>The four differ in <em>where</em> the split between space and time happens:</p><p>6pt</p><table class='xt'><tr><th>@l l &gt;p6.4cm@ spatio-temporal</th><th><strong>c</strong></th><th>no split at all — attention over every space-time token at once. Most expressive, quadratic in $n_t n_h n_w$</th></tr><tr><td>factorised encoder</td><td><strong>a</strong></td><td>split across <em>whole encoders</em>: a spatial encoder, then a temporal one</td></tr><tr><td>factorised self-attention</td><td><strong>d</strong></td><td>split <em>within each layer</em>: spatial attention, then temporal attention</td></tr><tr><td>factorised dot-product</td><td><strong>b</strong></td><td>split across <em>heads</em>: some heads attend spatially, others temporally, in the same operation</td></tr></table><p><strong>Order them by how coarse the split is</strong> — whole encoder, then layer, then head — and the three factorised variants stop blurring together.</p>",
