  "id": "e01q44",
  "exam": 1,
  "num": 44,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $\\mathbf{128 \\times 128 \\times 192}$</strong></p><p>Three steps, in order:</p><ol class='xl'><li><strong>Patch partition.</strong> $1024/4 = 256$, so Stage 1 operates on a $256 \\times 256$ grid with $C = 96$ channels.</li><li><strong>Patch merging</strong> concatenates each $2\\times2$ neighbourhood: spatial dimensions halve to $128 \\times 128$, channels <em>quadruple</em> to $4C = 384$.</li><li><strong>Linear projection</strong> halves that to $2C = 192$.</li></ol><p>Result: $\\mathbf{128 \\times 128 \\times 192}$.</p><p><strong>The pattern to remember is $2C$ per stage, not $4C$.</strong> Merging alone would quadruple the channels; the projection immediately halves it, so each stage doubles the width while quartering the spatial extent — which is the classic CNN pyramid shape, and exactly the hierarchy plain ViT lacks.</p>",
  "type": "numeric",
  "stem": "A Swin backbone receives a $1024\\times1024$ RGB image with patch size $P = 4$ and base channel dimension $C = 96$. What tensor shape enters Stage 2, i.e. after one patch-merging step, which halves the concatenated channel dimension?",
  "answer": "$\\mathbf{128 \\times 128 \\times 192}$",
  "expected": [
   128,
   128,
   192
  ]
