  "exam": 2,
  "num": 2,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Fusing at the raw input means concatenating the two signals <em>before</em> any encoder, and then running <em>one</em> network over the result. That only makes sense if a single operation is meaningful on both — same dimensionality, same units, same spatial layout. Two RGB cameras qualify. A camera and a LiDAR point cloud do not: there is no convolution that is simultaneously the right operation on a pixel grid and on an unordered set of $(x,y,z)$ points.</p><p><strong>Why (c) is the wrong answer even though it sounds right.</strong> Separate encoders per modality are exactly what <em>late</em> and <em>intermediate</em> fusion need. Requiring them would contradict the premise of the question.</p><p><strong>The single distinguishing question: how many vectors come out?</strong> One $\\Rightarrow$ fusion. One per modality $\\Rightarrow$ coordination. Everything else — early or late, attention or concatenation — is a detail inside one of these two shapes. This figure answers Q1, Q27 and Q46 at once.</p>",
