  "id": "e02q03",
  "exam": 2,
  "num": 3,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Plain bilinear fusion computes the outer product $a\\,b^{\\!\\top}$ and nothing else. Tensor fusion appends a constant $1$ to each modality vector <em>first</em>, and only then takes the outer product. That one change is the whole idea, because the appended $1$ manufactures three extra blocks for free.</p><p><strong>Reading it.</strong> The single interaction block is surrounded by both unimodal blocks and a constant, so a downstream linear layer can recover the image-only answer, the text-only answer, a bias, or any mixture — without the interaction having to encode them. Plain bilinear fusion has <em>only</em> the blue block, so if one modality is missing or uninformative the product collapses and nothing survives.</p><p><strong>4th true, and it is the method's fatal flaw.</strong> With $d$-dimensional vectors the two-modality output has $(d+1)^2$ entries; three modalities give $(d+1)^3$. At $d = 128$ that is $129^3 \\approx 2.1$ million entries for <em>one</em> fused representation. This is why low-rank factorisations of tensor fusion exist at all.</p><p><strong>3rd false.</strong> Tensor fusion is a fixed algebraic construction applied to two <em>already pooled</em> vectors. Cross-modal attention aligns <em>sequences</em> — which word attends to which image region. They solve different problems and are routinely used together.</p>",
