  "num": 6,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>3rd is false, and it is the point of the queue.</strong> If gradients flowed back through the queue entries you would need the computation graph of every one of them in memory — which is precisely the cost the queue was invented to avoid. Entries are detached feature vectors: cheap, and no longer differentiable.</p><p><strong>Which raises the problem the momentum encoder solves.</strong> If the key encoder were just the student's weights, then a vector computed five hundred steps ago would have been produced by a very different network, and comparing it to today's query would be meaningless. The EMA update $\\theta_k \\leftarrow m\\,\\theta_k + (1-m)\\,\\theta_q$ with $m \\approx 0.999$ makes the key encoder drift slowly enough that the whole queue stays <em>approximately consistent</em>.</p><p><strong>The decoupling in one line:</strong> SimCLR's number of negatives <em>is</em> its batch size, so buying more negatives means buying more GPU memory. MoCo's number of negatives is the queue length, which costs only the storage of $K$ vectors.</p>",
