  "exam": 2,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>This one is genuinely confusing the first time, because the output looks exactly like a classifier's.</p><p><strong>The situation.</strong> DINO has no labels, so it cannot have classes. But it needs the student and teacher to <em>agree</em>, and to train agreement by gradient descent you need a differentiable measure of disagreement between two outputs.</p><p><strong>The trick.</strong> Push both outputs through a softmax over $K$ dimensions. Now each is a probability distribution, and cross-entropy between them is a perfectly good loss. The $K$ dimensions are just the coordinates that make this possible — an arbitrary vocabulary the network invents for itself. Nothing says dimension $37$ means “dog”.</p><p><strong>The consequence you should be able to state.</strong> Because the dimensions are arbitrary, the trivial solution — put everything in dimension $37$ regardless of input — also achieves perfect agreement. That is collapse, and it is why centering and sharpening exist (Q28, Q47, Q57).</p>",
