  "explanation": "<p>Three of the four options are true statements. The question asks which is not a reason for <em>efficiency</em>, and that is a different test.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th>@c p11.4cm@ (a)</th><th><strong>is a reason, and the dominant one.</strong> $49$ tokens instead of $196$; attention is quadratic, so the encoder's attention cost falls by $16\\times$ (Q40).</th></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td><strong>is a reason.</strong> The decoder runs on all $196$ tokens, so it would be the new bottleneck if it were not deliberately shallow and narrow. It is thrown away afterwards, so nothing is lost by keeping it small.</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td><strong>is true but is <em>not</em> an efficiency reason.</strong> A per-pixel $L_2$ loss over $147$ patches rather than $196$ saves an utterly negligible amount of compute next to a Transformer forward pass. Restricting the loss to masked patches is about the <em>task</em> — scoring the model on patches it could simply copy would let it score well without predicting anything.</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td><strong>is a reason, and a real one.</strong> Needing no negatives is why MAE trains at ordinary batch sizes while SimCLR needs $8192$ (Q6).</td></tr></table>",
