  "exam": 2,
  "num": 12,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>The taxonomy is fixed by two questions, and the names follow mechanically.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th>@l c c l@ <strong>setting</strong></th><th><strong>tasks</strong></th><th><strong>target labels</strong></th><th><strong>also called</strong></th></tr><tr><td>inductive</td><td><em>differ</em></td><td>available</td><td>ordinary fine-tuning</td></tr><tr><td><strong>transductive</strong></td><td><strong>same</strong></td><td><strong>none</strong></td><td>unsupervised domain adaptation</td></tr><tr><td>unsupervised</td><td><em>differ</em></td><td>none</td><td>—</td></tr></table><p><strong>The 1st is false by definition.</strong> If the domains were also the same, then source and target would be the same distribution and the same task — there would be nothing to transfer.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is the one that decides the row.</strong> <em>Transductive</em> means labels on the source and <em>none</em> on the target; labelled data in both domains is the inductive setting, ordinary fine-tuning. That leaves: same task, different domain, no target labels — which is word-for-word the definition of unsupervised domain adaptation, so the 4th is true.</p>",
