  "exam": 2,
  "num": 13,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>The instinct is to mark all four, because LoRA is “the memory-saving method”. Being precise about <em>which</em> memory it saves is the whole question.</p><p><strong>1st, not removed.</strong> The frozen weights are still needed for every forward pass. They sit in VRAM exactly as before — LoRA does not shrink the model, it shrinks what you train.</p><p><strong>3rd and 4th, removed — and this is where the saving is.</strong> A frozen parameter has no gradient and no optimiser state. Adam keeps two states per trainable parameter, so the base model's three companion copies (gradient, first moment, second moment) simply vanish.</p><p><strong>2nd, not removed, and this catches people out.</strong> Activations must still be stored for the backward pass, because the gradient has to be propagated <em>through</em> the frozen layers to reach adapters sitting deeper in the network. Freezing a weight removes its gradient; it does not remove the need to backpropagate past it.</p><p><strong>$7$B parameters at $16$ bits</strong> (the arithmetic is Q42). The blue bar is identical in both rows — LoRA never touches it. The $4\\times$ figure in Q42 is exactly the ratio between the two rows, and the adapter's own footprint at rank $8$ is too small to see at this scale: $65\\,536$ parameters against $7$ billion.</p>",
