  "id": "e02q18",
  "exam": 2,
  "num": 18,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p>The update is \\[ x_t+1 = x_t + 2 _xp(x_t) + z_t, z_t N(0,I). \\] Delete the last term and what remains is <em>gradient ascent on</em> $\\log p$. Gradient ascent is a deterministic optimiser: it climbs to the nearest stationary point and stops.</p><p>Look at the figure above. Start anywhere left of the valley and noiseless Langevin walks uphill to mode A and sits there forever. It will never visit mode B, never produce a sample from the tail, and never produce two different outputs from the same start. You get the <em>mode</em>, not the <em>distribution</em> — and a generative model that always returns the single most likely image is useless.</p><p><strong>The noise is therefore not numerical hygiene; it is the entire difference between optimisation and sampling.</strong> It is also what carries a sample across the valley, since the score there points the wrong way.</p><p><strong>Why (b) is wrong.</strong> In the tails the score points strongly <em>inwards</em> (see the green curve at $x = \\pm 3$), so the iteration is self-correcting, not divergent.</p>",
