  "explanation": "<p>Write the update as a base point plus a correction: \\[ = _(x_t,)_<span>unconditional</span> + (_(x_t,c) - _(x_t,))_<span>the “direction of the condition”</span> \\] <strong>1st true.</strong> A single network is trained with the condition randomly dropped (say $10\\%$ of the time), so it learns both the conditional and the unconditional score. No separate classifier is trained, and none has to be made robust to noisy inputs — which was the awkward part of <em>classifier</em> guidance.</p><p><strong>2nd true, and this is the practical appeal.</strong> $\\gamma$ appears only at sampling time, so fidelity can be traded against diversity per sample, with no retraining.</p><p><strong>3rd true — substitute.</strong> At $\\gamma = 1$ the expression telescopes to $\\varepsilon_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}_t,c)$ exactly. So $\\gamma = 1$ is “no guidance, just conditioning”; $\\gamma = 0$ discards the condition entirely (Q43); $\\gamma > 1$ extrapolates <em>past</em> the conditional score, which is what sharpens prompt adherence and, pushed too far, produces the over-saturated look.</p><p><strong>4th true, and it is what makes the 1st possible.</strong> Dropping the condition at random during training — typically about $10\\%$ of examples — is the entire training-time change. It is what leaves one network able to produce both scores, and therefore what removes the separate classifier.</p><p><strong>And note the cost, which no option states:</strong> both $\\varepsilon_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}_t,c)$ and $\\varepsilon_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}_t,\\varnothing)$ must be evaluated at every sampling step — two forward passes, so guided sampling costs twice as much (Q43).</p>",
