  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p>\\[ T(t) = (-_t_n^t(r(s)) ds) \\] <strong>Every option is true, and the 2nd is the one the formula settles.</strong> $\\sigma \\ge 0$, so the integral is non-decreasing in $t$, so its negative exponential is non-<em>increasing</em>. A dropping density makes $T$ stop falling; it cannot make $T$ rise. Physically: light already absorbed does not come back.</p><p><strong>1st is the definition.</strong> $T(t)$ is the probability that a ray entering at $t_n$ has not been absorbed by the time it reaches $t$.</p><p><strong>3rd is the same fact from the other side.</strong> Where $\\sigma = 0$ nothing is added to the integral, so $T$ is flat — empty space is free.</p><p><strong>4th.</strong> $T(t)$ is the probability of getting <em>as far as</em> $t$; $\\sigma(t)\\,dt$ is the probability of being stopped in the next stretch given that you got there. Their product is the probability of terminating at $t$, and it is exactly the weight $w(t) = T(t)\\,\\sigma(t)$ that multiplies the colour in the rendering integral. The weights form a proper distribution along the ray, which is what makes hierarchical sampling possible: sample where the weight is large.</p><p><strong>Three stacked panels, one story.</strong> $T$ can only fall. It is flat through empty space, collapses through the object, and is then pinned near zero forever — so the weight $w = T\\sigma$ is large only in the thin shell where $\\sigma$ has just turned on while $T$ is still high. Samples <em>behind</em> the object have a perfectly good $\\sigma$ but are multiplied by $T \\approx 0$, so they are invisible. <strong>This is also the answer to Q59</strong>, which asks you to draw it.</p>",
