  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: encoder $\\mathbf{49}$ tokens, decoder $\\mathbf{196}$; attention term $\\mathbf{16\\times}$ smaller</strong></p><p><strong>Encoder:</strong> $196 \\times (1 - 0.75) = \\mathbf{49}$ tokens. <strong>Decoder:</strong> all $\\mathbf{196}$ — the $49$ encoded tokens plus $147$ mask tokens, because the output must have one prediction per patch position.</p><p><strong>The quadratic term.</strong> Attention costs $O(n^2)$: \\[ 196^249^2 = 38 4162 401 = 16, <span>i.e. </span> 4^2 . \\]</p><p><strong>The qualification that turns a correct answer into a good one.</strong> $16\\times$ is the reduction in the <em>attention</em> term only. The per-token feed-forward cost is linear in $n$, so it falls by just $4\\times$, and the decoder still processes all $196$ tokens. The end-to-end training speedup reported for MAE is therefore around $3\\times$, not $16\\times$. Quoting $16\\times$ as the overall speedup is the standard error here.</p>",
