  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $\\mathbf{65{,}536}$ parameters $=\\mathbf{0.39\\%}$ of the matrix</strong></p><p>\\[ 2dr = 2 4096 8 = 65 536 <span>against</span> d^2 = 4096^2 = 16 777 216 . \\] \\[ 65 53616 777 216 = 1256 = 0.39% . \\] The factor $\\tfrac{1}{256}$ is worth recognising directly: it is $2r/d = 16/4096$. The adapter's size scales <em>linearly</em> in $d$ while the matrix scales quadratically, so the saving gets better as models get wider.</p><p><strong>One honest caveat.</strong> This is one matrix. A real LoRA fine-tune adapts the query and value projections in every layer — say $2$ matrices $\\times$ $32$ layers — so the trainable total is $64 \\times 65\\,536 \\approx 4.2$ million. Still under $0.1\\%$ of a $7$B model, but not $65\\,536$.</p>",
