  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $\\mathbf{92{,}160{,}000}$ evaluations per frame</strong></p><p>\\[ 800 600_480 000 <span>rays</span> (64 + 128)_192 <span>samples/ray</span> = 92 160 000 92 <span>million</span>. \\] One MLP evaluation per sample, one ray per pixel. This single number is the reason NeRF cannot render in real time, and the reason 3DGS — which evaluates <em>no</em> network — was such a jump (Q23, Q39, Q54).</p><p><strong>A detail worth knowing, though not required by the question as worded.</strong> In the original NeRF the fine network does not evaluate $128$ new points in isolation; it evaluates the <em>union</em> of the coarse and fine samples, $64 + 128 = 192$ points, while the coarse network has already evaluated its own $64$. That gives $256$ evaluations per ray and $480\\,000 \\times 256 = 122\\,880\\,000$ per frame. If you wrote that and said why, it is the better answer.</p>",
