  "explanation": "<p>Unrolling the recurrence puts the same Jacobian $\\partial h_t/\\partial h_{t-1}$ into the chain once per step, so the gradient reaching step $t-k$ carries a factor that behaves like $\\lambda^{k}$, where $\\lambda$ is the dominant singular value. Anything but $\\lambda \\approx 1$ is exponential.</p><p><strong>Note the vertical axis is logarithmic</strong>, so these straight lines are exponentials. At $k = 20$, $\\lambda = 0.6$ has already shrunk the gradient to $3.7\\times10^{-5}$ while $\\lambda = 1.4$ has grown it to $837$. Even $\\lambda = 0.9$ — almost harmless-looking — is down to $5\\times10^{-3}$ by $k = 50$.</p><p><strong>Both failures are the same mechanism with the parameter on either side of $1$</strong>, which is why they are taught together and why only one of them has a cheap fix (Q50).</p>",
