  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: global $\\mathbf{9{,}834{,}496}$ pairs; windowed $\\mathbf{153{,}664}$; ratio $\\mathbf{64\\times}$</strong></p><p>$N = 56 \\times 56 = 3\\,136$ patches.</p><p><strong>Global:</strong> every patch attends to every patch, $3136^2 = \\mathbf{9\\,834\\,496}$ pairs.</p><p><strong>Windowed:</strong> $(56/7)^2 = 64$ windows of $49$ patches, each costing $49^2$: $64 \\cdot 2\\,401 = \\mathbf{153\\,664}$ pairs.</p><p>\\[ <span>ratio</span> = 9 834 496153 664 = 64 = <span>the number of windows</span>. \\]</p><p><strong>That the ratio equals the window count is not a coincidence</strong>, and seeing why is the real content. Splitting $N$ tokens into $W$ equal windows leaves each window with $N/W$ tokens and $(N/W)^2$ pairs; times $W$ windows that is $N^2/W$. So the saving is always exactly the number of windows — and it grows as the image does, while the cost per window stays fixed. That is the sense in which window attention is linear (Q33).</p>",
