  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $256^3 \\approx \\mathbf{67}$ MB; $512^3$ is $\\mathbf{8\\times}$ that, $\\approx \\mathbf{537}$ MB</strong></p><p>$256^3 = 16\\,777\\,216$ voxels at $4$ bytes $= 67\\,108\\,864$ bytes $\\approx \\mathbf{67}$ MB.</p><p>Moving to $512^3$ multiplies the count by $2^3 = \\mathbf{8}$: $134\\,217\\,728$ voxels, $\\approx \\mathbf{537}$ MB.</p><p><strong>Put that in context and the problem is obvious.</strong> $537$ MB is for a single grid holding one scalar per voxel. A network stores activations for every layer, times the batch size, and $512^3$ is still a coarse resolution for a room — roughly $1$ cm voxels over $5$ metres. Meanwhile the surface those voxels describe is two-dimensional, so the occupied fraction falls as the resolution rises: refining the grid spends memory overwhelmingly on empty space. This one calculation is the motivation for octrees, sparse convolutions and point-based networks alike (Q42, Q54).</p>",
