  "explanation": "<p>sample $P$ classes, $K$ images each the batch is <em>structured</em>, not random\\ $e_i \\leftarrow \\phi(x_i)$ for all $i$ in the batch\\ $L \\leftarrow 0$\\ <strong>for</strong> each $a$ in the batch: every sample is an anchor once\\ *1.5em$p^{*} \\leftarrow \\arg\\max_{p:\\,y_p = y_a,\\ p \\ne a}\\ \\mathcal{D}(e_a,e_p)$ <em>furthest</em> positive\\ *1.5em$n^{*} \\leftarrow \\arg\\min_{n:\\,y_n \\ne y_a}\\ \\mathcal{D}(e_a,e_n)$ <em>nearest</em> negative\\ *1.5em$L \\mathrel{+}= \\max\\bigl(0,\\ \\mathcal{D}(e_a,e_{p^{*}}) - \\mathcal{D}(e_a,e_{n^{*}}) + m\\bigr)$\\ $L \\leftarrow L / (PK)$</p><p><strong>Note which extreme is taken on each side</strong>, because it is easy to write one of them backwards: the <em>hardest positive</em> is the one furthest away, the <em>hardest negative</em> the one closest. Both are the cases the current embedding handles worst, which is the definition of hard.</p><p><strong>$PK$ terms, not $2\\,688$.</strong> One triplet per anchor, against batch-all's every combination (Q48) — and because each is chosen to be the hardest available, each is almost certainly active rather than clipped to zero by the hinge.</p><p><strong>The one-sentence answer about global mining.</strong> Searching the whole dataset for the nearest negative returns the globally hardest examples, which in any real dataset means label noise and near-duplicates — unsolvable triplets whose loss cannot be reduced by learning, so the optimiser takes the collapse instead (Q58); restricting the search to the batch keeps the triplets hard enough to be informative and easy enough to be solvable, and re-randomising the batch each step varies which ones appear.</p>",
